{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"OK, I now have a comprehensive understanding. The `debug_skill.md` has `{{sense_skill_path}}` (1 occurrence). So if I inline debug_skill content, I'd need to replace that. But that's part of the doing/easy flow, not plan.\n\nFor the plan command, the key skills are grilling, tdd-zh, testing-anti-patterns-zh (all 0 `{{`).\n\nNow let me design the final implementation cleanly.\n\n**Step 1: Add `ReadEmbeddedSkill` to `internal/prompt/manager.go`.**\n\n```go\n// skillPath maps a skill name to its embedded path under templates/skills/.\nfunc skillPath(name string) string {\n\treturn \"templates/skills/\" + name + \".md\"\n}\n\n// ReadEmbeddedSkill reads a single embedded skill's raw content by name.\n// It is the cross-package accessor for the unexported skillsFS (builder 是独立包，\n// skillsFS 未导出，必须经本导出函数访问)。Missing skill returns an error rather\n// than silently producing empty content.\nfunc ReadEmbeddedSkill(name string) (string, error) {\n\tcontent, err := skillsFS.ReadFile(skillPath(name))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"embedded skill %q not found: %w\", name, err)\n\t}\n\treturn string(content), nil\n}\n```\n\nThen refactor `LoadCoreSkills` to use `skillPath` for consistency:\n```go\nfunc LoadCoreSkills(names []string) string {\n\tvar parts []string\n\tfor _, name := range names {\n\t\tcontent, err := skillsFS.ReadFile(skillPath(name))\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tlog.Printf(...)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\t...\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nThis keeps `LoadCoreSkills` behavior identical (just refactored to use skillPath).\n\n**Step 2: Add a helper in builder to build inline skill sections.**\n\nIn `pibuilder.go`, add a helper:\n```go\n// renderInlineSkills reads embedded skills via prompt.ReadEmbeddedSkill and renders\n// a single-file cohesive \"skill 内联段\" section. Missing skill returns error.\nfunc renderInlineSkills(skills []string) (string, error) {\n\tvar sb strings.Builder\n\tfor _, name := range skills {\n\t\tcontent, err := prompt.ReadEmbeddedSkill(name)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t\t}\n\t\tsb.WriteString(\"\\n\\n### skill:\")\n\t\tsb.WriteString(name)\n\t\tsb.WriteString(\"\\n\\n\")\n\t\tsb.WriteString(stripYAMLFrontmatter(content))\n\t\tsb.WriteString(\"\\n\")\n\t}\n\treturn sb.String(), nil\n}\n```\n\nWait, should I strip the YAML frontmatter? The grilling.md doesn't have frontmatter (it starts with `# Skill: Grilling`). The tdd-zh.md has frontmatter `---\\nname:...\\n---`. For the inline section, stripping frontmatter makes sense (it's a method content, not a standalone file). Actually, let me check: grilling.md starts with `# Skill: Grilling（结构化追问协议）` — no frontmatter. tdd-zh.md starts with frontmatter. testing-anti-patterns-zh.md starts with `# 测试反模式` — no frontmatter.\n\nSo for tdd-zh, I should strip frontmatter to avoid including the YAML `---` in the inline section (which could confuse pi). Actually, stripping frontmatter is consistent with how `renderDoingLoop` strips frontmatter from doing_loop.md.\n\nBut wait — does stripping matter for the test? The test greps for \"Grilling|结构化追问\". grilling.md has no frontmatter, so it doesn't matter for grilling. For tdd-zh, frontmatter has `name: test-driven-development` — if I don't strip, the inline section would have a stray `---` block. That's harmless but ugly. I'll strip frontmatter for cleanliness.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether to strip. The `stripYAMLFrontmatter` function is defined in both `prompt` (easy_prompt.go) and `builder` (pibuilder.go). In builder, it's:\n```go\nfunc stripYAMLFrontmatter(s string) string {\n\tif !strings.HasPrefix(s, \"---\\n\") {\n\t\treturn s\n\t}\n\trest := s[4:]\n\tidx := strings.Index(rest, \"\\n---\\n\")\n\tif idx < 0 {\n\t\treturn s\n\t}\n\treturn strings.TrimLeft(rest[idx+5:], \"\\n\")\n}\n```\n\nThis works fine. I'll use it.\n\n**Step 3: Modify `BuildPlan` to inline skills.**\n\n```go\nf